syntax:
Constructor declaration within a class:

```c++
class Sales_data {
public:
    Sales_data() = default; // Default constructor
    Sales_data(const std::string &s) : bookNo(s) {} // Constructor taking a string
    Sales_data(const std::string &s, unsigned n, double p) : bookNo(s), units_sold(n), revenue(p) {} // Constructor taking multiple arguments
    Sales_data(std::istream &is); // Constructor taking an input stream

    // ... other member functions ...
private:
    std::string bookNo;
    unsigned units_sold = 0; // In-class initializer
    double revenue = 0.0;  // In-class initializer
};
```

**Constructor definition outside the class:**
```c++
Sales_data::Sales_data(std::istream &is) {
    is >> bookNo >> units_sold >> revenue;
}
```

**Notes:**
* Constructors do not have a return type.
* Member initializers are specified in a constructor initializer list using the colon (:) after the constructor parameters.
* Members not initialized in the initializer list are default-initialized or initialized using in-class initializers.
* Using a constructor initializer list is generally preferred for efficiency, especially for non-POD types and when dealing with const members.
* Default constructors are crucial for dynamic memory allocation or creating objects that can be later initialized

